  "worked_solution": "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+P(x){y}^{\u2032}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{\u2032}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{\u2032}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{\u2032}(x)=-2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{\u2032} & =-2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|W| & =-2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 5 & =C \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5{e}^{-2\\cdot 3}=5{e}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
